  "exact_tex_lf": "\\begin{remark}\n\\label{remark-flat-pairs-not-two-out-of-three}\n\\begin{reference}\n\\cite[Appendix, nos. 22--23]{GAGA}\n\\end{reference}\nCall an inclusion of rings $R \\subset S$ a \\emph{flat pair} if $S/R$ is a\nflat $R$-module. Flat pairs compose: if $A \\subset B \\subset C$ and\n$(A, B)$ and $(B, C)$ are flat pairs, then $(A, C)$ is a flat pair.\nIndeed, Lemma \\ref{lemma-flat-ses} shows that $B$ is flat over $A$ and\nthat $C$ is flat over $B$, and hence $C$ is flat over $A$ by\nLemma \\ref{lemma-composition-flat}. The inclusions $A \\to B$ and\n$B \\to C$ are universally injective by\nLemma \\ref{lemma-flat-universally-injective}; hence $A \\to C$ is universally\ninjective by Lemma \\ref{lemma-composition-universally-injective}.\nApplying Lemma \\ref{lemma-ui-flat-domain} to\n$0 \\to A \\to C \\to C/A \\to 0$ shows that $C/A$ is flat over $A$.\n\nThe other two-out-of-three implication fails. Let $k$ be a field, put\n$B = k[t]$ and $C = B \\times k$, and use the inclusions\n$$\nk = A \\longrightarrow B \\longrightarrow C,\n\\qquad\na \\longmapsto a,\n\\qquad\nb \\longmapsto (b, b(0)).\n$$\nBoth $(A, B)$ and $(A, C)$ are flat pairs because their quotients are\n$k$-vector spaces. On the other hand, the $B$-linear map\n$$\nC \\longrightarrow k,\n\\qquad\n(b, c) \\longmapsto c - b(0)\n$$\nhas kernel $B$ embedded as above. Thus $C/B \\cong k = B/(t)$ as a\n$B$-module. This module is not flat: tensoring the injection $(t) \\to B$\nwith $B/(t)$ gives the zero map from the nonzero module\n$(t) \\otimes_B B/(t) \\cong B/(t)$ to $B/(t)$. See\nLemma \\ref{lemma-flat}. Therefore $(B, C)$ is not a flat pair.\n\\end{remark}\n",
    "counterexample_verification": [
      "The graph map k[t] to k[t] x k, b maps to (b,b(0)), is an injective unital ring map and extends the constant inclusion k=A to B.",
      "B/A and C/A are k-vector spaces, hence flat A-modules, so (A,B) and (A,C) are flat pairs.",
      "Give k the B-module structure by evaluation at t=0. The B-linear map q:C to k, q(b,c)=c-b(0), is surjective and its kernel is exactly the graph copy of B.",
      "Therefore C/B is isomorphic to k=B/(t) as a B-module.",
      "The ideal (t) is a free rank-one B-module. Tensoring (t) into B with B/(t) produces the zero map from (t) tensor_B B/(t), which is isomorphic to B/(t) and is nonzero. By tag 00HD, B/(t) is not flat.",
      "Hence (B,C) is not a flat pair, giving precisely the asserted non-converse."
    ],
